  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "C.Shekar Gudipati vs Through The Sho Of Lalapet P.S, on 1 October, 2019 Author: U. Durga Prasad Rao Bench: U. Durga Prasad Rao Hon'Ble Sri Justice U. Durga Prasad Rao Criminal Petition No.6033 Of 2019 Order:",
    "facts": "1. In this Criminal Petition, filed under Section 482 of Cr.P.C., the Petitioner/A1 seeks to quash the proceedings against him in Crime No.215 of 2019 of Lalapet Police Station, Guntur Urban, which was registered against him and others for the offences under Sections 188, 272, 273, 328 and 420 IPC and Sections 58, 59, 63 & 64 of Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 (for short, 'the FSS Act'). 2. The allegations against the Petitioner/A1 and other accused, briefly, are that on 29.06.2019 at 03.00 p.m., when the complainant along with his staff including the mediators rushed to Peddapalli Sandhu, Lalapet Area, Guntur town, found the petitioners/A1 to A7 possessing the banned tobacco products, and on interrogation as the accused voluntarily confessed to have been engaged in the business of prohibited tobacco products, seized the same, registered F.I.R., remanded them to judicial custody for the offences stated supra.",
    "arguments": "3. Learned counsel for petitioner while denying the FIR allegations would submit that even if the prosecution case is accepted to be true, tobacco products would not fall within the definition of Section 3(j) of FSS Act and therefore the police are not authorized to register the crime, investigate and file charge sheet and therefore registration of the FIR is contrary to the provisions of FSS Act. As can be seen, in the said UDPR,J common order a learned Judge of this Court partly allowed the Criminal Petitions and [LEAKAGE_MASK] the offences punishable under Indian Penal Code and FSS Act.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "3. Learned counsel for petitioner while denying the FIR allegations would submit that even if the prosecution case is accepted to be true, tobacco products would not fall within the definition of Section 3(j) of FSS Act and therefore the police are not authorized to register the crime, investigate and file charge sheet and therefore registration of the FIR is contrary to the provisions of FSS Act.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "As can be seen, in the said UDPR,J common order a learned Judge of this Court partly allowed the Criminal Petitions and [LEAKAGE_MASK] the offences punishable under Indian Penal Code and FSS Act."
  },
